{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 43, :comments ["<b>And the land shall be deserted, etc.; because, and because, etc.</b> The verse's saying ״יַעַן וּבְיַעַן״ is in the manner of what it says (Jeremiah 9:12): ״עַל מָה אָבְדָה הָאָרֶץ עַל עָזְבָם אֶת תּוֹרָתִי״, and they of blessed memory said (Bava Metzi'a 85b): This matter was asked, etc., until the Holy One, blessed be He, came and explained it: ״וַיֹּאמֶר ה׳ עַל עָזְבָם אֶת תּוֹרָתִי״. The meaning is, the question is that if Israel sinned, what did the land sin that it was destroyed? For the Master of the judgment should have exacted payment from them and the land would not have been destroyed, and its destruction is through the departure of its children from within it and the entry of the uncircumcised and the impure into it, which is the annihilation of the land. ״וַיֹּאמֶר ה׳ עַל עָזְבָם אֶת תּוֹרָתִי״ — see, the departure of Israel from the land of Israel is for the cause of preventing Torah, for since there is no Torah, the land has no desire for their dwelling in it. And we find further that the verse said above this: ״אָז תִּרְצֶה וְגוֹ׳ וְתִרֶץ אֶת שַׁבְּתוֹתֶיהָ״ — see, that on account of the sin of Shemittah the land is taken from them. From now on these are two reasons for the departure of Israel from the Holy Land." "<b>Therefore</b> it says ״יַעַן וּבְיַעַן״, and it went back and explained what the two causes are, and said: <b>they rejected My ordinances</b> — these are the Shemittot; <b>and their soul abhorred My statutes</b> — this is the matter of Torah, as it says at the beginning of the parashah: ״אִם בְּחֻקֹּתַי״, and they explained it as referring to the matter of Torah. And from the implication of the matter you know that both causes are required, for if they were engaged in Torah they would be chastised in a different manner and the land would be appeased for the nullification of Shemittah on account of the benefit of the Torah heard in it. And if they fulfilled the commandment of Shemittah, even though they were not engaged in Torah, HaShem would chastise them in a different manner and they would not go into exile. And when both are combined, the judgment emerged to bring them into the land of their enemies, as it said at the beginning of the matter."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:02:42.813605220Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}